<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">SriLankan operates a mixed fleet, and which aircraft you're booked on changes the experience more than almost anything else about the flight, so it's worth checking your specific aircraft type before you fly if seat quality matters to you.</span></p>
<h2><strong>The A321neo Business Class Cabin</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">On regional routes, SriLankan flies the Airbus A321neo, with a 12-seat Business cabin arranged 2-2 across three rows.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">This isn't a lie-flat product, since it's built for shorter regional sectors rather than long-haul overnight flights, but reviewers consistently note that legroom is excellent for a narrowbody cabin and the seats are colourful and well maintained.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Entertainment varies by aircraft age: newer A320neo-family jets have larger back-of-seat screens, while older aircraft use a personal screen that folds out from inside the armrest. There's no Wi-Fi in this cabin.</span></p>
<h2><strong>The A330-200: SriLankan's Older Long-Haul Product</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The A330-200 carries SriLankan's older-generation Business seats in a 2-2-2 layout across three rows.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">It's a genuine lie-flat seat, but window passengers don't get direct aisle access, and reviewers describe the seats as comfortable enough but a bit firm for sleeping.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">There's no Wi-Fi on this cabin either, and compared to the newer product, it offers noticeably less privacy.</span></p>
<h2><strong>The A330-300: SriLankan's Flagship Business Product</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">This is where SriLankan's Business Class actually competes. The A330-300 features 28 seats in a 1-2-1 reverse herringbone configuration, meaning every single passenger gets direct aisle access, a meaningful upgrade over the A330-200's 2-2-2 layout.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">A 2024 cabin refurbishment updated the interior with the airline's signature peacock and cream colour palette, eco-friendly materials including recycled carpets and headrest covers, and a starry-night ceiling effect designed to help with sleep on longer sectors.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">For more information on the cabin and onboard services, see the</span><a href="https://www.srilankan.com/en_uk/flying-with-us/business-class"> <span style="font-weight: 400;">SriLankan Airlines Business Class details</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400;">.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">That said, independent reviews are mixed on how the seat itself holds up against 2026 competition.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The A330-300 uses a Safran Cirrus reverse herringbone seat, one of the earlier iterations of this seat family, and it delivers on the fundamentals (fully flat, direct aisle access).</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Some reviewers describe it as one of the more basic versions of the reverse herringbone concept, with limited head privacy and modest storage space compared to newer products from Qatar Airways, Cathay Pacific, Japan Airlines, or Finnair.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">It's worth noting, too, that not every A330-300 in the fleet has received the 2024 refresh yet, so the specific aircraft you're assigned can still turn up the older-generation cabin, something more than one reviewer has discovered only after boarding.</span></p>
<h2><strong>Food and Drink</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">This is consistently one of the strongest parts of the SriLankan Business Class experience.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Expect traditional Sri Lankan dishes alongside international options, served with champagne, a solid spirits selection, and what reviewers repeatedly call an impressive tea menu, a nod to Sri Lanka's tea-growing heritage.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Amenity kits aren't always distributed on shorter regional sectors, but essentials like socks, a toothbrush, toothpaste, and an eyeshade are typically offered from a basket, along with dental and shaving kits and Acca Kappa aftershave oil in the lavatory on longer flights.</span></p>
<h2><strong>Lounge Access</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Business Class passengers connecting through SriLankan's home hub get access to the Serendib Lounge at Bandaranaike International Airport in Colombo, which includes shower facilities, a real advantage if you're connecting onward after a long-haul sector.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">You can also find the</span><a href="https://www.airport.lk/passenger_guide/transit/airline_lounges"> <span style="font-weight: 400;">Bandaranaike International Airport lounge information</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400;"> for details on available airport lounges.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Lounge access at other airports depends on local agreements and your specific routing, so it's worth checking what's available at your departure and connection points before you fly, particularly on partner-operated segments.</span></p>
<h2><strong>In-Flight Entertainment and Wi-Fi</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Entertainment quality tracks closely with aircraft type. Newer aircraft in the fleet offer larger screens and a broader content library, while older jets rely on smaller, dated screen setups.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Wi-Fi remains a genuine gap in the product: it's not available on the A330-200 and inconsistent across the rest of the fleet, so if staying connected in the air matters to you, this is worth factoring into which route and aircraft you book.</span></p>
<h2><strong>How SriLankan Business Class Compares</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">For a oneworld carrier flying largely regional and medium-haul routes to Asia, the Middle East, and a handful of long-haul connections to Europe and Japan, SriLankan's Business product does what it needs to for its typical 3 to 11-hour sectors, without pretending to compete at the very top of the market.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">On the London-to-Colombo route specifically, it's often compared favourably to older-generation Business products from carriers like British Airways and Royal Jordanian, while sitting behind the newer offerings from Qatar Airways, Finnair, and the major East Asian carriers.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">For a detailed seat-by-seat breakdown of the fleet, the</span><a href="https://www.australianfrequentflyer.com.au/srilankan-airlines-business-seat-guide/"> <span style="font-weight: 400;">Australian Frequent Flyer seat guide to SriLankan Airlines</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400;"> covers every current configuration in depth, and One Mile at a Time's A330 Business Class review offers a detailed first-hand look at the flagship long-haul seat.</span></p>
<h2><strong>Baggage and Ground Experience</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Business Class tickets come with a meaningfully higher baggage allowance than Economy:&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">40kg checked, versus 30kg in Economy, plus two cabin bags at 7kg each rather than one.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Business passengers also get priority check-in and boarding, which is a genuine time-saver at Colombo's Bandaranaike Airport during peak departure banks.</span></p>
<h2><strong>Is SriLankan Airlines Business Class Worth It?</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">The honest answer depends heavily on which aircraft you're booked on. If you land the refreshed A330-300, you're getting a genuinely competitive product for the route, with direct aisle access, a real flat bed, and standout food and drink.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">If you end up on the A330-200 or an unrefurbished A330-300, you're getting a comfortable but dated seat that does the job without impressing.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Since fare prices don't typically distinguish between these cabins upfront, it's worth checking your specific aircraft type at booking, or shortly before departure, so your expectations match what you'll actually find on board.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">If Business Class isn't within reach for an upcoming trip, particularly for students travelling to or from Sri Lanka for study, it's worth checking a current</span><a href="https://lankaairlines.discount/srilankan-airline-student-discount/"> <span style="font-weight: 400;">SriLankan Airlines student discount</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400;"> before assuming Economy is your only option, since discounted student fares can sometimes bring a more comfortable booking within budget.</span></p>
<h2><strong>Conclusion</strong></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">SriLankan Airlines Business Class is a genuinely solid product when you land the refreshed A330-300, with strong food, a real flat bed, and a lounge experience that holds its own for a regional oneworld carrier.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Where it falls short is fleet consistency: older A330-200 cabins and unrefreshed A330-300s still make up part of the network, and Wi-Fi remains a real gap across most of the fleet.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Check your aircraft type before you book if the seat itself is the deciding factor for you.</span></p>
